<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-10-21.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="203537"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#203537" label="[20:35:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: Hi, so we mostly have riscv64 userspace bootstrap path mapped out and working, but not fully implemented in live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="203603"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#203603" label="[20:36:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">probably userspace bootstrap should be finished before we think about kernel bootstrap...</span></div><div class="line" id="203708"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#203708" label="[20:37:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for kernel, we need some early kernel and intermediate kernel as you have said</span></div><div class="line" id="203731"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#203731" label="[20:37:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps we can reuse (unfinished) stage0-uefi stuff for early baremetal bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="204006"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#204006" label="[20:40:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">If I remember correctly uefi/x86_64 I was able to bootstrap up to tcc-mes (which sometimes segfaults on x86_64 userspace bootstrap too, though a bit later... Anyway my &quot;kernel&quot; would need more features before tcc-mes works)</span></div><div class="line" id="204146"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#204146" label="[20:41:46]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">pure non-UEFI bootstrap would probably need to be done on a per SoC basis...</span></div><div class="line" id="210333"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210333" label="[21:03:33]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: Hi. Is there a place where I can look at riscv64 bootstrap path and see what is working and what is not?</span></div><div class="line" id="210608"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210608" label="[21:06:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: I can share some documentation, it's still a bit raw but better than nothing</span></div><div class="line" id="210613"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210613" label="[21:06:13]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">As for early kernel - I see only amd64 support in stage0-uefi so I am not sure how reusing it is different from reusing stage0-posix with riscv port of builder-hex0 project</span></div><div class="line" id="210801"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210801" label="[21:08:01]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: yeah, I would really like to see any documentation. Should I provide you with an email address to send it or you can just send link?</span></div><div class="line" id="210825"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210825" label="[21:08:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://stikonas.eu/files/bootstrap/lb-gcc9.txt">https://stikonas.eu/files/bootstrap/lb-gcc9.txt</a>  (early steps, only up to musl despite lb-gcc9 in the name)</span></div><div class="line" id="210846"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#210846" label="[21:08:46]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">thanks</span></div><div class="line" id="210922"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#210922" label="[21:09:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a bit more here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://stikonas.eu/files/bootstrap/gcc_musl_wip.txt">https://stikonas.eu/files/bootstrap/gcc_musl_wip.txt</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="211216"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211216" label="[21:12:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">basically most of the live-bootstrap path works (I haven't tested perl / autotools but presumably they should work)</span></div><div class="line" id="211304"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211304" label="[21:13:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: then for GCC 4 we use the following backport by ekaitz: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/ekaitz-zarraga/gcc">https://github.com/ekaitz-zarraga/gcc</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="211328"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211328" label="[21:13:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">efraim: is also working on integrating that in Guix</span></div><div class="line" id="211351"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211351" label="[21:13:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: builder-hex0 is kind of the same, that kernel is somewhat similar to porting it to C</span></div><div class="line" id="211401"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211401" label="[21:14:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but what I'm thinking is that riscv doesn't have BIOS</span></div><div class="line" id="211422"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211422" label="[21:14:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you can't just write something portable that can print to script and do disk operations</span></div><div class="line" id="211451"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211451" label="[21:14:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you either need to write builder-hex0 kind of thing specific to your board</span></div><div class="line" id="211511"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211511" label="[21:15:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or we can assume UEFI (which in practive likely to be U-boot but does't matter I guess)</span></div><div class="line" id="211534"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211534" label="[21:15:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but anyway, nothing is complete there...</span></div><div class="line" id="211555"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211555" label="[21:15:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so it's up to people who do work to decide</span></div><div class="line" id="211626"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211626" label="[21:16:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: also bootstrappable tcc has a small missing bit</span></div><div class="line" id="211640"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211640" label="[21:16:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we have a version that works on riscv64 but not on x86 and vice versa</span></div><div class="line" id="211648"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211648" label="[21:16:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it would be nice to fix it, so it works on both</span></div><div class="line" id="211718"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211718" label="[21:17:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: see <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gitlab.com/janneke/tinycc/-/commit/ea3900f6d5e71776c5cfabcabee317652e3a19ee">https://gitlab.com/janneke/tinycc/-/commit/ea3900f6d5e71776c5cfabcabee317652e3a19ee</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="211738"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211738" label="[21:17:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this revert breaks riscv64 and restores x86 support</span></div><div class="line" id="211947"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211947" label="[21:19:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is probably the main blocker for non-hacky upstreaming to live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="211948"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#211948" label="[21:19:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so the commit that works on riscv is <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gitlab.com/janneke/tinycc/-/commit/dd46e018f64cbd67d907ce6ea91923e627206363">https://gitlab.com/janneke/tinycc/-/commit/dd46e018f64cbd67d907ce6ea91923e627206363</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="212455"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#212455" label="[21:24:55]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm not sure wheter api that uefi gives will be sufficient (tbh, never looked at uefi). If i recall corectly, in raw machine (or supervisor) mode on risc-v even interaction with interrupt controller could differ a little from board to board so portability could be a problem</span></div><div class="line" id="212939"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#212939" label="[21:29:39]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but I guess we can have builder-hex0 kind of thing that requires only little tweaks (e.g. adding disk operations and interrupts controller handlers at bottom of file) in order to work on specific board</span></div><div class="line" id="213132"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#213132" label="[21:31:32]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tcc problem looks interesting, maybe I will take a look at it</span></div><div class="line" id="213257"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#213257" label="[21:32:57]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and about gcc 4 backport - is it functional? (Can it compile things that are used later in the bootstrap process?)</span></div><div class="line" id="213259"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213259" label="[21:32:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, UEFI gives us file I/O (so higher level than disk i/o), output to screen (potentially keyboard input too but I haven't looked at it)</span></div><div class="line" id="213407"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213407" label="[21:34:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: yes, gcc 4 backport can compile at least binutils and gcc 9</span></div><div class="line" id="213455"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#213455" label="[21:34:55]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, okay, then assuming UEFI sounds really convenient</span></div><div class="line" id="213501"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213501" label="[21:35:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">M2libc already has UEFI support, so that's good in principle, though early hex stages of course are somewhat different</span></div><div class="line" id="213523"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213523" label="[21:35:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">UEFI API is harder to use than POSIX API</span></div><div class="line" id="213528"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213528" label="[21:35:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so needs more code to do I/O</span></div><div class="line" id="213618"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213618" label="[21:36:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I suggest, let's start with finishing userspace bootstrap, then do UEFI, and if you like you could then bootstrap pure machine...</span></div><div class="line" id="213639"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#213639" label="[21:36:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(be back in a bit, dinner)</span></div><div class="line" id="213647"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#213647" label="[21:36:47]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Isn't translating POSIX API calls into UEFI API calls what early kernel supposed to do?</span></div><div class="line" id="213851"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#213851" label="[21:38:51]">&lt;timafrolov&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Bon appetit. I probably will go sleep by the time you return, so if you have anything more to say - I will read it in logs in the morning.</span></div><div class="line" id="220027"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220027" label="[22:00:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: yes, but given the complexity of writing hex code for riscv64, it's probably easier to postpone kernel itself till you have C</span></div><div class="line" id="220031"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220031" label="[22:00:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even builder-hex0 does minor cheating, it kind of is written in hex2 and then gets autotranslated into well commented hex0</span></div><div class="line" id="220033"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220033" label="[22:00:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since it is fairly large (4 KiB or so)</span></div><div class="line" id="220034"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220034" label="[22:00:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and significant chunk of functionality is only needed for later post-C stages</span></div><div class="line" id="220210"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220210" label="[22:02:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">now builder-hex0 has a small early stage that knows hex0 and can build much larger 4KiB kernel (real builder-hex0)</span></div><div class="line" id="220253"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220253" label="[22:02:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess if somebody would start writing x86 builder-hex0 from scratch, one could instead do early loader that then loads next small loader that knows hex1 and then next one  that knows hex2 and that could build 4 KiB kernel from hex2. But oh well, I don't think this will happen now</span></div><div class="line" id="220856"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#220856" label="[22:08:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: well, basically posix-runner now does exactly that, translates POSIX API calls to UEFI API calls, though it misses some other things that kernel really needs...</span></div><div class="line" id="221002"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#221002" label="[22:10:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">timafrolov: in particular I need to add at least a bit of support for paging, right now only relocatable binaries work, and on UEFI we can't put child processes in hardcoded locations</span></div><div class="line" id="221057"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#221057" label="[22:10:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(of course that stuff is all very platform specific, riscv64 kernel would need quite a bit of porting)</span></div><div class="line" id="221224"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#221224" label="[22:12:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">alternatively, we could read all files into memory shut down UEFI services and then we have more control</span></div><div class="line" id="221359"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#221359" label="[22:13:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but we would have to implement other parts of the kernel then, basic FS should be easy enough, probably directly port from builder-hex0. But we would also have to deal with display framebuffer, etc...</span></div><div class="line" id="223612"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223612" label="[22:36:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, builder-hex0 has it easy because it can (ab)use the VGA BIOS &amp; VGA text modes - in the UEFI world, and especially on arm/riscv, &quot;text mode&quot; doesn't exist</span></div><div class="line" id="223717"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223717" label="[22:37:17]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(UEFI allows, but doesn't require, exposing native text modes pre-ExitBootServices)</span></div><div class="line" id="223836"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223836" label="[22:38:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">all those additional features: paging, fs and framebuffer output would basically make it a proper kernel rather than UEFI program...</span></div><div class="line" id="223909"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223909" label="[22:39:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but yes, builder-hex0 has some things easy...</span></div><div class="line" id="223927"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223927" label="[22:39:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so it can act as a kernel without any support for paging or framebuffer</span></div><div class="line" id="224437"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224437" label="[22:44:37]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And most importantly, no need to embed a font</span></div><div class="line" id="224631"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224631" label="[22:46:31]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Even without BIOS, it could get away with directly writing the &quot;native&quot; VGA text mode &quot;frame&quot; buffer, something you can't do on systems without VGA compatibility</span></div><div class="line" id="224804"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224804" label="[22:48:04]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">At the same time, it's kind of cheating from a free software standpoint: after all, what is the license of the font embedded in your VGA controller's hardware? (You usually can't know.)</span></div><div class="line" id="225035"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225035" label="[22:50:35]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's irrelevant</span></div><div class="line" id="225037"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#225037" label="[22:50:37]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's hardware</span></div><div class="line" id="231455"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231455" label="[23:14:55]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'd argue it's relevant in this case, as the hardware manufacturer might try to abuse copyright to claim that the bootstrap's visual output is owned by them</span></div><div class="line" id="231523"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231523" label="[23:15:23]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although it certainly doesn't affect Trusting Trust-resistance</span></div><div class="line" id="233839"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233839" label="[23:38:39]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Jeremiah Orians&gt; Well fonts could be simple if we are ok with ascii only for the early bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="233916"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233916" label="[23:39:16]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Jeremiah Orians&gt; (Or petscii</span></div><div class="line" id="233916"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233916" label="[23:39:16]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> :-p. )</span></div><div class="line" id="235837"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#235837" label="[23:58:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ascii is probably sufficient for bootstrap</span></div><br /></div></body></html>